# The bare-metal deploy runner: a host the Ansible role can converge on.
#
# `AGENTS.md` calls bare metal via Ansible the only canonical deployment shape,
# because it is the only one where the sandbox actually works — and until
# ISSUE-439 it was the only shape with no tier that ran it. Fourteen
# `tests/test_ansible_*.py` files parse the role's YAML and assert on the parse;
# nothing in the repository ever executed `ansible-playbook`. So the role's
# systemd units, its `uv sync`, its rclone mount and the ordering between them
# were covered by reading the YAML and by deploying to production.
#
# `scripts/test-deploy.sh` is the only supported way to run this — see that
# script for the seven container settings it needs, the reason for each, and
# why they must never appear in a deployment compose file.
# `tests/deploy/conftest.py` owns the container and drives `deploy/install.sh`
# inside it.
#
# **This is deliberately not `docker/test/Dockerfile`, and the difference is
# PID 1.** That image runs with `--init`, which puts tini at PID 1 so orphaned
# grandchildren get reaped — chosen precisely because, as its own comment says,
# "on a real host systemd does this job". A container whose PID 1 is a reaper is
# the one container the role cannot converge on: every `systemctl enable` and
# `systemctl start` in it needs systemd as PID 1 with a writable cgroup tree.
#
# PID 1 is now the *whole* difference: that image moved to `python:3.12-slim-
# trixie` in ISSUE-440, so both are Debian 13 and the bases no longer diverge.
# Said explicitly because the reverse used to be a second reason to keep the
# two files apart, and a reader who remembers that would otherwise conclude
# the split had stopped paying for itself.
FROM debian:13-slim

# `container=docker` is what systemd itself reads to know it is not booting a
# machine: it skips the hardware-adjacent units and stops trying to own things
# the kernel is not giving it. Without it the boot is slower and noisier and
# leaves failed units that have nothing to do with the role.
ENV container=docker
ENV DEBIAN_FRONTEND=noninteractive
ENV LANG=C.UTF-8

# **Almost nothing is installed here, and that is the point of the tier.**
# `deploy/install.sh` apt-installs python3, pipx, ansible-core and git itself,
# and the role installs everything else. Baking any of that into a layer would
# mean the tier stopped covering the step that installs it — which is most of
# what a bare-metal installer *is*. So this image carries systemd, the two
# things the installer's own pre-flight needs before it can install anything
# (`curl` for its connectivity check, `ca-certificates` so that check and every
# later download can verify a certificate), and nothing else.
#
# `systemd-sysv` is what puts `/sbin/init` on the path `CMD` names. `dbus` is
# not decoration: `systemctl` talks to PID 1 over it, so without it every
# `systemctl` call in the role fails to connect and the converge dies on the
# first unit.
RUN apt-get update && apt-get install -y --no-install-recommends \
    systemd systemd-sysv dbus ca-certificates curl \
    && rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/*

# Mask the units that have no meaning in a container and would otherwise sit in
# `systemctl --failed` for the whole session. That matters more than tidiness
# here: `tests/deploy/test_bare_metal_deploy.py` asserts on the *whole* failed
# set rather than on named units, because a converge that leaves some unit
# broken is exactly what this tier exists to notice, and an assertion naming
# only the units we thought of cannot see the one we did not. Masking is what
# makes that whole-set assertion meaningful instead of permanently red.
#
# `systemctl mask` rather than deleting `.wants` symlinks by hand: mask is a
# single documented operation with one spelling, and the `find`-and-`rm` form
# in circulation has broken `-o` precedence that silently deletes the wrong set
# or exits non-zero on a path the base image happens not to ship.
RUN systemctl mask \
      systemd-udevd.service \
      systemd-udev-trigger.service \
      systemd-firstboot.service \
      systemd-resolved.service \
      getty.target \
      console-getty.service

# SIGRTMIN+3 is systemd's "start an orderly shutdown". `docker stop` sends
# SIGTERM by default, which systemd reads as "isolate the default target" and
# so does not stop the container — the daemon then waits out its whole timeout
# and SIGKILLs, which loses the journal the tier reads on a failure.
STOPSIGNAL SIGRTMIN+3

CMD ["/sbin/init"]
